#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186128819 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "516c2e89-2bce-5636-b16d-aa045d9f9c31" :knowledge-point/title "Finding Elements of a Relation Using a Recursive Formula"}] :question/id #uuid "e1c7a206-77db-55e2-8b0e-cde3e9f1b489" :question/math-academy-id "e-16539" :question/problem "Find the fourth term of the sequence given by the recurrence relation\n$a_n = 5a_{n-1}+a_{n-2}, \\qquad a_1 = 2, \\quad a_2 = 1, \\quad n \\geq 3$." :question/worked-solution "Starting with the initial terms $a_1 = 2$, and $a_2 = 1$, we compute the next terms using the recursive formula until we reach the fourth term.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\na_{1} &= 2 \\\\\na_{2} &= 1 \\\\\na_{3} &= 5a_{2} + a_{1} = 5 \\cdot 1 + 2 = 5 + 2 = 7 \\\\\na_{4} &= 5a_{3} + a_{2} = 5 \\cdot 7 + 1 = 35 + 1 = 36\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the fourth term is $a_4 = 36$."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186128820 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "b4ade459-9aba-5520-95d3-69ef3fd62d9d" :knowledge-point/title "Classifying Recurrence Relations"}] :question/id #uuid "17f9c357-b103-5a51-95ad-ad0ea0b1fba9" :question/math-academy-id "e-16541" :question/problem "Which of the following linear recurrence relations are homogeneous?\n\n1. $a_n = a_{n-1} + 5a_{n-2}+n$\n2. $b_n = 3b_{n-1} -2b_{n-2}$\n3. $c_n = 2c_{n-1} + 3c_{n-2}- c_{n-3}$" :question/worked-solution "A linear recurrence relation (with constant coefficients) is **homogeneous** if the forcing function is zero.\n\nIn other words, the linear recurrence relation\n\n$$\na_n = s_1a_{n-1} + s_2a_{n-2} + \\ldots + s_ka_{n-k} + g(n)\n$$\n\nis homogeneous if $g(n) = 0$ for all $n$, and $s_i$ is constant for each $i$.\n\nLet's examine each recurrence relation in turn.\n\n- $a_n$ is not homogeneous. The term $n$ of the expression doesn't contain preceding terms of the relation.\n- $b_n$ is homogeneous. The terms of the expression each contain a preceding term, $b_{n-1}$ and $b_{n-2}$.\n- $c_n$ is homogeneous. The terms of the expression each contain a preceding term, $c_{n-1}$, $c_{n-2}$, and $c_{n-3}$.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II and III only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186128821 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "2f1c9d93-fcb3-51d2-bc82-badf49d45de7" :knowledge-point/title "Fully Classifying Recurrence Relations"}] :question/id #uuid "493a826d-4f6f-55e2-9d6a-a0909d34a483" :question/math-academy-id "e-16555" :question/problem "Fully classify the following recurrence relation:\n$a_n = a_{n-2} - \\dfrac23 a_{n-1}, \\qquad a_1 =0, \\quad a_2 = 1, \\quad n \\geq 2$." :question/worked-solution "Recall the following classifications of recurrence relations:\n\n- The order of a relation $a_n$ is the largest index difference between $a_n$ and the preceding terms in the relation.\n- A recurrence relation is *linear (with constant coefficients)* if it expresses $a_n$ as a sum of previous terms $a_{n-1}, \\dots, a_{n-k}$ with constant coefficients, together with possibly a final term that depends only on $n{:}$\n$a_n = s_{1}a_{n-1} + s_{2}a_{n-2} + \\ldots + s_{k}a_{n-k} + g(n)$\nwhere $s_1, s_2, \\ldots, s_k$ are constants and $g(n)$ is a function, often called a *forcing function*, that does not involve $a_n$ or its preceding terms.\n- A linear recurrence relation (with constant coefficients) is *homogeneous* if the forcing function is zero. Otherwise, it is *inhomogeneous.*\n\nLet's classify the relation\n\n$$\na_n = a_{n-2} - \\dfrac23 a_{n-1}\n$$\n\n- The largest index difference between $a_n$ and the preceding terms $a_{n-1}$ and $a_{n-2}$ in the relation is $2$. So, the relation has order $2$.\n- The previous terms $a_{n-1}$ and $a_{n-2}$ occur only in the first power and their coefficients are constants. So, the relation is linear.\n- The terms of the expression each contain a preceding term, $a_{n-1}$ and $a_{n-2}$. So, the relation is homogeneous.\n\nTherefore, the recurrence relation $a_n$ has order\n$\\bbox[3pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{blue}2}$,\nis\n$\\bbox[3pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{blue}\\text{linear}}$,\nand is\n$\\bbox[3pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{blue}\\text{homogeneous}}$."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129508 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "516c2e89-2bce-5636-b16d-aa045d9f9c31" :knowledge-point/title "Finding Elements of a Relation Using a Recursive Formula"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "8c4529d2-0223-539a-84d3-7092b35719fd" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "24" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186126835} {:answer/id #uuid "ae5feb8b-f9e5-5920-96e0-1fb160219341" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "41" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186126836} {:answer/id #uuid "09f9aa59-f57a-5d9e-a005-f8606e5cc298" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "32" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186126837} {:answer/id #uuid "1ff68374-0b27-55d1-8491-135021da6c3f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "35" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186126838} {:answer/id #uuid "3718cdf7-b4db-5d90-bb3d-6365fad82a5f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "19" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186126839}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "1ff68374-0b27-55d1-8491-135021da6c3f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "35" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186126838} :answer-field/id #uuid "1b45c6fd-dfb0-5465-a53a-87f5f66006ba" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186126834}] :question/id #uuid "1b8ee369-c2d6-57ab-995b-02dacf3c692c" :question/math-academy-id "q-252990" :question/problem "Find the fifth term of the sequence given by the recurrence relation\n\n$a_{n} = 2a_{n - 1} - 3, a_{1} = 5, n \\ge 2$."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129510 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "b4ade459-9aba-5520-95d3-69ef3fd62d9d" :knowledge-point/title "Classifying Recurrence Relations"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choic